                <pb facs="0840" n="832" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg832"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/2365: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-11-13T17:00:00+08:00">November 13, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 13—12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1241. My 1228, November 9, 3 p.m.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d895-f1"/> My French
                    colleague in response to an inquiry made by me this afternoon stated that the
                    two Chinese courts are now functioning practically in a normal manner and that
                    out of a total of 22 judges on the 2 courts only 2 or 3 have refused to sit
                    under the new authority. The French authorities seemed to be entirely satisfied
                    with the manner in which the courts are being administered.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Chungking, Peiping, code text to Tokyo by air
                    mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
